REGEX (BASIC AND EXTENDED)
==========================
[The ^ metacharacter restricts match to start of line]
[The $ metacharacter restricts match to end of line]
LINE ANCHORS
sed -n '/^s/p' anchors.txt      (find all lines starting with 's')
sed -n '/rt$/p' anchors.txt     (find all lines ending with 'rt')
sed 's/^par$/PAR/'              (change only the line 'par' to 'PAR')
Note: Duplicates only arise when you use the /p (print character)
sed 's/^/*/'                    (insert * at the beginning of every line)
sed '/ /! s/$/./'               (append '.' if a line doesnt contain any spaces)
WORD ANCHORS
**The escape sequence \b denotes a word boundary. This works for both the start of word and the end of word anchoring.**
**Word boundaries are at first/last character, number, or underscore**
sed -n '/\bpar/p' anchors.txt   (print lines with words that start with par)
sed -n '/par\b/p' anchors.txt   (print lines with words that end with par)
sed 's/\bpar\b/PAR/' anchors.txt  (replace only word 'par' in the text)
sed -n 's/\bpar\b/***/p' anchors.txt    (replace and print only lines with word 'par')
sed -n '/\Bpar\B/p' anchors.txt (print lines where 'par' is surrounded by chars only)
sed -n '/\Bpar/p' anchors.txt   (print lines that contain 'par' but NOT as start of a word)
sed -n '/par\B/p' anchors.txt   (print lines that contain 'par' but NOT at the end of a word)
printf 'alphanumeric\n' | sed 's/\b/:/g'  (add a ':' at every word boundary)
printf 'alphanumeric' | sed 's/\B/:/g'    (anywhere there is NOT a word boundary (inside) add :)
ALTERNATION BETWEEN OPTIONS
sed -n '/two\|sub/p' anchors.txt  (print lines containing 'two' or 'sub' Basic regex with escape)
sed -nE '/two|sub/p' anchors.txt  (print same lines with extended regex, no escape)
sed -E 's/cat|dog|fox/--/g'   (replace any occurance of 'cat', 'dog', or, 'fox' in a line with --)
sed -nE '/\bpar\b|s$/p' anchors.txt       (lines with the whole word 'par' or ending with 's')
NOTE: The alternative which matches earliest in the input gets precedence.
printf 'cats dog bee parrot\n' | sed -E 's/dog|parrot|at/--/'
printf 'cats dog bee parrot\n' | sed -E 's/dog|parrot|at/--/'
(both above give the same c--t dog bee parrot)
Note: If the matches start from the same place the longest match gets precedence
printf 'understanding\n' | sed -E 's/under|understanding|understand/--/' (replace longest with --)
GROUPING SIMILAR WORDS 
When what you want to match shares letter you can group them using a pair of parentheses metacharacters. Similar to a(b+c)d = abd+acd in maths, you get a(b|c)d = abd|acd in regular expressions.  printf 'red\nreform\nread\ncrest\n' | sed -nE '/reform|rest/p'    (print lines with reform/rest)
printf 'red\nreform\nread\ncrest\n' | sed -nE '/re(st|form)/p'    (print same lines with grouping)
sed -nE '/\bpar(|t)\b/p' anchors.txt      (print lines containing words 'par'/'part' with grouping)
MATCHING THE METACHARACTERS
NOTE: To match the metacharacters literally, i.e. to remove their special meaning, prefix those characters with a \ character.
printf 'a^2 + b^2 - c*3\nd = c^2\n' | sed -n '/b^2/p' (line anchors not in usual place dont matter)
printf '$a + $b = $c\n$x = 4' | sed -n '/$b/p'        (another example, anchor char doesnt matter)
printf '(a/b) + c\n3 + (a/b) - c\n' | sed -n '/^(a\/b)/p' (escaping a forward slash in basic regex)
printf '(a/b) + c\n3 + (a/b) - c\n' | sed -nE '/^\(a\/b\)/p' (escaping slash/bracket extended regex)
USING DIFFERENT DELIMITERS
The / character is idiomatically used as the REGEXP delimiter. But any character other than \ and the newline character can be used instead. The syntax is simple for substitution and transliteration commands, just use a different character instead of /.
printf 'some text here\n' | sed -n 's# text#thing#p'  (using the # as the delimeter instead of /)
printf 'some text here\n' | sed -n 's* text*thing*p'  (same idea as above)
echo 'a/b/c/d' | sed 'y,a/d,1-4,' (used in place of echo 'a/b/c/d' | sed 'y/a\/d/1-4/')
Note: For address matching, syntax is a bit different — the first delimiter has to be escaped. 
printf '/home/joe/1\n/home/john/1\n' | sed -n '\@joe@p'   (here im using @ for the delimeter)
THE DOT META CHARACTER
The dot metacharacter serves as a placeholder to match any character (including the newline character).
echo 'tac tin cot abc:tyz excited' | sed 's/c.t/-/g'    (using the dot metacharacter for differences)
printf '42\t3500\n' | sed 's/.3.//'         (shows how . can represent a tab \t character also)
QUANTIFIERS
NOTE:Alternation helps you match one among multiple patterns. Combining the dot metacharacter with quantifiers (and alternation if needed) paves a way to perform logical AND between patterns.
NOTE:First up, the ? metacharacter which quantifies a character or group to match 0 or 1 times. 
echo 'fed fold fe:d feeder' | sed -E 's/\bfe.?d\b/X/g' (using the ? metachar to isolate different wd)
sed -nE '/\bpart?\b/p' anchors.txt    (print lines with par/part in them using extended regex ?)
echo 'par part parrot parent' | sed -E 's/par(ro)?t/X/g' (use extended regex to select two words)
echo 'par part parrot parent' | sed -E 's/par(ro|en)?t/X/g' (replace several words with (),? exreg)
echo 'apple \< fig ice < apple cream <' | sed -E 's/(\\)?</\\</g' (make all \< and escape \'s)
NOTE: The * metacharacter quantifies a character or group to match 0 or more times.
echo 'fd fed fod fe:d feeeeder' | sed 's/fe*d/X/g' (f followed by 0 or more e's followed by d)
echo '3111111111111125111142' | sed -E 's/1+4?2/-/g' (one or more 1's, optional 4, then 2 replaced -)
NOTE: You can specify a range of integer numbers, both bounded and unbounded, using {} metacharacters. There are four ways to do this {m}, {m,} {,n}, {m,n}.
echo 'ab abbba abbbba abbbbbba' | sed -E 's/ab{2,4}a/X/g' (replace ab(2-4 times)a by X using range)
NOTE: With ERE, you have escape { to represent it literally. Unlike ), you don't have to escape the } character.
echo 'report_{a,b}.txt' | sed -E 's/\{a,b}/c/'  (escape the range character { )
CONDITIONAL AND
NOTE: Construct AND conditional using the dot metacharacter and quantifier.
echo 'Error: not a valid input' | sed -n '/Error.*valid/p'  (Error and followed by valid using .*)
echo 'two dogs and a cat' | sed -E 's/cat.*dog|dog.*cat/pets/' (cat + dog in any order, needs altern)
echo '12313213helloworld!' | sed -E 's/(1|2|3)+hello/X/' (the + beside an alternation-do until fails)
LONGEST MATCH WINS
echo 'car bat cod map scat dot abacus' | sed 's/.*b/-/' (start of line to last b replaced by -)
echo 'car bat cod map scat dot abacus' | sed 's/t.*u/-/' (first t in the line to last u, rep by -)

